# Studio roles ≠ Cursor Tasks

## Hard rule

**Do not spawn a Cursor Task (or chat persona) as PM, Critic, Spokesman,
Builder, Reviewer, Curator, Capacity Steward, or any other studio role.**

Those roles exist only as queue-driven executors on the **host worker**
(`runtime/roles/*`, claimed via `runtime.worker`). Spawning = **enqueue a
task** (e.g. `pm.tick`, `work.*`), then ensure the worker claims it.

## What “spawn PM” means

1. Enqueue `pm.tick` (or the appropriate type) with the goal/payload.
2. Confirm a host worker is running (`python -m runtime.worker` / compose
   service).
3. Report the task id and status. Wait on the queue — do not role-play PM.

The studio PM **plans and enqueues work**; it **never implements** product
code. If you write `gateway/` / features while calling yourself PM, you have
already violated the role (see
`state/lessons/2026-07-27-cursor-task-is-not-studio-pm.md`).

## Allowed Cursor agent uses

- **Off-host helper (ADR-0010):** research, docs, code on an explicit
  `builder/<summary>` (or other non-role-impersonating) branch when the
  stakeholder asked this session to implement, or when delegated via
  `state/offhost/`.
- **Separate review agent** for a merge-to-`main` review round (CONTRIBUTING)
  — that is a process role for git review, not `runtime/roles/pm.py`.
- **Never** title or prompt a Task “You are the PM/Spokesman/…” and have it
  drive studio execution.

## If tempted to fake a role

Stop. Enqueue. Start/fix the worker. Say so. Do not substitute a Cursor
subagent for the runtime.
